{"parentUuid":"13167c5e-0393-4a13-8fc3-1bf03b61245f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgG6WQRCtwzQwMZxE9bzv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm picturing a button that morphs via shared layout into a \"Create New\" panel with a close button and a grid of icon tiles, but spotting several issues in that design: missing click handlers and button types on grid items, no escape/outside-click dismissal or focus management, layoutId morphing across mismatched elements, missing aria attributes, using index as key, default state created inside render, an exit opacity flash, and a layout spring that distorts the text since children aren't getting their own layout treatment.\n\nNow I'm looking at where our actual Add button lives — the sidebar footer, full-width at 211px, currently opening a centered capture modal with five options (Note, Link, File, Quote, Screenshot). I want to replace this with a disclosure pattern where the button morphs upward into the options panel, so I'm weighing whether the panel should stay within the narrow 211px sidebar width or overflow rightward over the library frame — leaning toward anchoring it bottom-left and letting it grow to around 280px wide, overlapping the frame edge, though I need to check how that interacts with the frame sliding behavior.\n\nSince the frame currently sits above the sidebar, I need the panel to get a higher z-index so it doesn't get covered, which should work since the sidebar's absolute positioning has no competing stacking context. I also want to check the capture modal's existing code to see how the multi-step flow works, like what happens after someone picks \"Link\" and needs to paste a URL, so the new panel preserves that same interaction.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3173,"cache_read_input_tokens":1